{{define "filespec" -}}
1. Dockerfile: a single build stage FROM {{.BaseImage}}, every version
   pinned, no COPY or ADD instructions, no secrets, layers ordered
   stable-first so rebuilds stay cache-friendly: system packages first,
   then language toolchains, then fast-moving tools.
2. manifest.json: a JSON array with one item per installed tool. Each
   item has "name", "version", "reason" (plain language: why it is
   needed), "start_line" and "end_line" (the 1-based inclusive span of
   Dockerfile lines that install it), and "check_command" (a shell
   command whose output must contain the version).
{{- end}}

{{define "contract" -}}
Scope: toolchains only. Record language runtimes with their exact
versions, version managers, developer CLIs, and the system libraries the
stack needs. Never record dotfiles, shell theming, credentials, or
personal files, and never read credential stores such as keychains,
.netrc, .ssh, .aws, or browser profiles.

Translate every finding to its {{.BaseImage}} equivalent instead of
copying it. Homebrew packages and darwin binaries must become apt
packages or the tool's official Linux installer.

Write exactly two files into the current directory:

{{template "filespec" .}}

Keep the item count honest: include only what this machine actually
uses, not everything it has ever installed.
{{- end}}

{{define "inventory" -}}
You are preparing a Linux container image that mirrors this machine's
development toolchains. Inspect the machine, then write the two files
described below into the current directory. Do not install anything on
this machine and do not change any file outside the current directory.

{{template "contract" .}}
{{- end}}

{{define "repo" -}}
You are preparing a Linux container image for the project in this
repository. Derive what the project needs from the repository's own
files: manifests, lockfiles, toolchain version files, and CI configs.
The repository defines the target, not the machine running this scan;
ignore what happens to be installed here.

If .devcontainer/devcontainer.json exists, treat it as the strongest
statement of intent: translate its image choice and features into
Dockerfile instructions.

Never modify, create, or delete repository files. Read them only.

Write exactly two files, Dockerfile and manifest.json, into
{{.OutputDir}}:

{{template "filespec" .}}

Keep the item count honest: include only what the project actually
needs, not every tool it might ever touch.
{{- end}}

